+      'Retry safely fails the same way until WinGet lists the publisher\'s current version. Check App updates again after the next update check.',
+      'Automatic updates don\'t try the same installer again after it fails this check. It tries again when the catalog offers a different version or WinGet corrects the checksum.',
+      'Other failed automatic updates are tried again at the next check. If an app\'s automatic update fails 3 times in a row, automatic updates turn off for that app and its card shows Auto-update off. Installers that fail the check before packaging don\'t count toward the 3. Update still works.',
+      'When that happens, a Teams alert, Auto-update turned off for the app, posts to IT Ops once. Press Open Deployments in the alert to read the failures.',
+      'After fixing the cause, press Turn auto-update back on on the app\'s card, or choose Auto-update in its menu or in the Auto-update on list. This also clears the failed count.',
+      'If Update says the catalog has not synced the installer yet, try again later. IntuneGet looks for a newer catalog every 6 hours.',
+      'To deploy it sooner, open the app in the App catalog, choose the new version and press Deploy again. It replaces the current copy in Intune with the groups shown in the panel.',
+      'Custom apps (installers that are not in WinGet, such as PdfCraft or UniFi Endpoint): open the app in Inventory and press Deploy new version under Actions.',
+      'The form is filled in from the last version. Enter the new version and its https download link. Computers update in place, as with Update.',
+      'Like Update, the new version takes the groups the previous copy has in Intune when it deploys, with Intune\'s default assignment settings, and leaves that copy with none. The Deployment list marks it Replaces current copy. IntuneGet removes the previous copy later.',
+      'When a custom app installed from a GitHub release has a newer release, App updates shows it. Deploy new version opens the form with the new version, download link and checksum filled in.',
+      'Check them, then add it to the Deployment list and deploy it from there. Nothing deploys on its own.',
+      'Custom apps from other sites, such as UniFi Endpoint, can\'t be checked. Watch the vendor\'s releases.',
+      'Deploy new version shows only on the newest version IntuneGet deployed, so an older copy is never used by mistake.',
+      'A custom app\'s first version is added with Custom app in the App catalog.',
+      'Before deploying, IntuneGet warns if an app has no groups: it would be added to Intune but install nowhere.',
+      'If the deployment says the app is already in Intune and asks whether to deploy the new version as its replacement, that is the normal step for a new version.',
+      'Check the groups it lists. If it says Supersedes, the button reads Deploy (new version) and replace (old version). If it says Doesn\'t supersede, the button reads alongside instead.',
+      'Deployed alongside, both versions stay in Intune, and IntuneGet never removes the old one. Use duplicate cleanup in Inventory if you want only one.',
+      'For WinGet and curated apps, open the app in Inventory and press Find in App catalog under Actions. The App catalog opens already searching for the app, so you can deploy a newer version.',
+      'Inventory reads Intune again when you come back to the tab and its list is over a minute old.',
+      'When Update, an automatic update or a custom app\'s new version carries groups to the new copy, the new copy gets Intune\'s default assignment settings: notifications show all, and delivery optimization is not configured. Exclusion groups carry over too.',
+      'For Available groups, the old copy\'s setting to automatically update superseded versions isn\'t copied to the new copy.',
+      'Availability dates, installation deadlines and restart grace aren\'t copied either. Set them again in Inventory with Availability and deadline on the new copy.',
+      'If the old copy has no groups, or IntuneGet can\'t read them from Intune, nothing is assigned to the new copy and the old copy keeps its groups. Home flags it.',
+    ],
+    links: [{ label: 'Open App updates', href: '/dashboard/updates' }, { label: 'How old copies are removed', href: '/dashboard/help#old-copies' }],
+  },
+  {
+    id: 'old-copies',
+    title: 'How old copies are removed',
+    uiLabels: ['Needs attention', 'needs a look', 'old app copies need a look', 'Open app', 'Open Inventory', 'Inventory', 'Keep this copy', 'Delete from Intune', 'Add a second copy anyway', 'Deploy as new app anyway', 'App activity', 'Removed by IntuneGet: old copy after an update', 'Go back to previous version', 'Install results', 'Groups', 'Previous copy', 'Kept for review'],
+    flow: ['A new copy replaces it', 'Wait 7 days after replacement', 'Newest copy installs well', 'Update check confirms conditions', 'Take groups off, remove links', 'Delete the old copy'],
+    points: [
+      'Each update, and each Deploy new version of a custom app, adds a new copy to Intune that replaces the previous copy. The previous copy stays in Intune until IntuneGet removes it.',
+      'An ordinary update moves the previous copy\'s groups to the new copy, with Intune\'s default assignment settings (see Updating an app). After a pilot\'s wider rollout, the previous copy keeps its groups too.',
+      'At the 3:30 AM and 3:30 PM update checks (Maryland time), IntuneGet removes those old copies of apps it deployed, once the checks below pass.',
+      'It removes an old copy only when all 5 of these are true:',
+      'The old copy was replaced at least 7 days ago, counted for each old copy from when the next copy was deployed. Apps updated more than once a week are still cleaned up.',
+      'The newest copy has at least 1 successful install, and no more than 1 in 10 of its install results are failures.',
+      'No pilot, retirement, going back or deployment of the app is under way.',
+      'The newest copy supersedes the old copy in Intune, directly or through the copies in between.',
+      'Every group on the old copy is also on the newest copy, with the same assignment type (Required, Available or Uninstall) and filter.',
+      'To remove a copy, IntuneGet takes its groups off, removes the links between it and the app\'s other copies, then deletes it from Intune. Deleting can\'t be undone.',
+      'Nothing is uninstalled from computers. The newest copy has already replaced the old one on them.',
+      'It removes at most 20 copies at each update check. The rest wait for the next check.',
+      'Apps used with the startup installer Beta are excluded from automatic old-copy cleanup, because a task staged on a computer may still refer to a copy. Confirm cancellation before manually cleaning up those copies.',
+      'It never touches copies added in Intune by hand, or removal copies ending in (uninstall only). Use duplicate cleanup in Inventory for those (see Deploying an app).',
+      'If it can\'t remove a copy, the copy stays in Intune and Home lists it under Needs attention as Old copy of [app] (v[version]) needs a look, with the reason. Press Open app to open the newest copy in Inventory.',
+      'Reasons include: the old copy has groups the newest copy lacks, another app replaces or depends on it, or Intune refused the change.',
+      'Home also lists a replaced copy that is due for removal while the newest copy, out 7 days or more, isn\'t installing well. Nothing is removed while that lasts.',
+      'The reason then says the newest copy has no groups, has no successful installs after 7 days, or that more than 1 in 10 of its installs failed.',
+      'If the newest copy has no groups, use Keep this copy on it in Inventory to move the old copy\'s groups to it, then check its Groups. Otherwise, open the newest copy and check Install results.',
+      'A copy held because the newest copy had no groups or no successful installs is removed at the first update check after a computer installs the newest copy.',
+      'A copy held because more than 1 in 10 installs failed is removed once failures are 1 in 10 or fewer. In both cases the other checks must still pass, and the copy then drops off Home.',
+      'A copy the newest one doesn\'t supersede, such as one deployed alongside, with Add a second copy anyway or with Deploy as new app anyway, is left alone and isn\'t listed on Home.',
+      'With more than one, Home shows [n] old app copies need a look and names up to 3, without reasons. Press Open Inventory, then open each newest copy to see why.',
+      'If the old copy has groups the newest lacks, use Keep this copy on the newest copy in Inventory, then Delete from Intune on the old one. Once deleted, it drops off Home.',
+      'Each removal shows in the app\'s App activity, marked Removed by IntuneGet: old copy after an update.',
+      'Go back to previous version still works after old copies are removed. It deploys the earlier installer again as a new copy, and doesn\'t use the old copy in Intune.',
+      'IT can turn automatic removal off on the server with the setting HRM_OLD_COPY_CLEANUP=off. To ask for this, open a ticket with HRM Support.',
+      'In Inventory, an older copy of an app IntuneGet deployed shows a Previous copy tag. Its details say Newer copy v[version] deployed [date].',
+      'They also say IntuneGet removes it 7 days after that if the newest copy installs well and has its groups.',
+      'If cleanup is holding the copy, the tag and the details say Kept for review, with a warning icon, and give the reason. Home lists the same copy under Needs attention.',
+      'If cleanup never removes the app\'s copies, such as startup Beta apps or with cleanup turned off, the details say only which newer copy was deployed, and when.',
+      'A second copy deployed on purpose can also show Previous copy. Cleanup never removes it, because the newer copy didn\'t replace it. To keep only one, use Keep this copy, then Delete from Intune.',
+      'Previous copies waiting for removal are left out of the copies listed at the top of Inventory. A copy cleanup is keeping stays there, so you can use Keep this copy.',
+    ],
+    links: [{ label: 'Open Inventory', href: '/dashboard/inventory' }, { label: 'HRM Support', href: HRM_SUPPORT_URL }],
+  },
+  {
+    id: 'pilot-rollouts',
+    title: 'Pilot rollouts',
+    uiLabels: ['Test with a pilot group', 'Find a pilot group', 'Deploy to pilot group', 'Pilot rollouts', 'Review results and wider rollout', 'No successful installations reported', 'New version currently reaches', 'New version after approval', 'I reviewed the results and approve these wider groups', 'Approve wider rollout', 'End failed pilot', 'End stalled start'],
+    flow: ['Open App updates', 'Choose pilot group', 'Deploy to pilot', 'Review both versions', 'Check wider groups', 'Approve wider rollout'],
+    points: [
+      'In App updates, choose Test with a pilot group on a deployed WinGet or curated app. Pilots are not available for custom apps or Microsoft Store apps.',
+      'In Find a pilot group, type at least 2 characters, select 1 small security group, then press Deploy to pilot group.',
+      'Deploy to pilot group and Approve wider rollout work only while the update schedule allows updates, no blackout applies and automatic updates are not paused (see Update schedule and blackouts).',
+      'The new version is Required for that group only. The previous copy keeps its groups. Apps with saved New computer setup profiles cannot use pilots yet.',
+      'During an active pilot, other updates and deployments for the app are held, including Update all, automatic updates and deploying it from the App catalog. Save to Intune and duplicate cleanup are held for its copies too. Self-updating apps can still update themselves.',
+      'Retire app and Go back to previous version are refused during a pilot too.',
+      'The Pilot rollouts card, under the App updates title, shows only while a pilot is running. Wait for the pilot deployment to finish, then press Review results and wider rollout.',
+      'Compare install results for both versions and read any failures before deciding.',
+      'The counts describe each whole app. They do not check pilot group membership or show individual devices. No successful installations reported means you do not yet have evidence of a successful pilot.',
+      'Compare New version currently reaches with New version after approval. The proposed groups come from the previous copy in Intune, including Required or Available, exclusions, filters and assignment settings.',
+      'To proceed, select I reviewed the results and approve these wider groups, then press Approve wider rollout. Nothing reaches the wider groups on its own. If the groups changed after your review, close it and open a fresh review.',
+      'Approval replaces the temporary pilot group with the previous copy\'s current groups on the new version. It does not uninstall the app from pilot devices. The previous copy keeps its groups, including if approval fails.',
+      'After wider rollout, IntuneGet removes the previous copy at an update check, once it was replaced 7 or more days ago and the newest copy installs well (see How old copies are removed).',
+      'If packaging fails or is held, open Deployments to inspect it. End failed pilot releases the hold only after the server confirms an unsuccessful finished workflow and no copy from that pilot remains in Intune.',
+      'A pilot held by the installation test before packaging starts can also be ended, after checking no copy exists. If a failed pilot copy remains, inspect and remove that copy in Intune before pressing End failed pilot.',
+      'If a start stalls before packaging begins, wait 2 minutes, then press End stalled start. Packaging that arrives after that is refused.',
+      'If the server cannot confirm that a failed pilot is safe to end, the hold remains. Use HRM Support if the deployment details do not explain what to do next.',
+    ],
+    links: [
+      { label: 'Open App updates', href: '/dashboard/updates' },
+      { label: 'Open Deployments', href: '/dashboard/uploads' },
+      { label: 'Open Inventory', href: '/dashboard/inventory' },
+      { label: 'HRM Support', href: HRM_SUPPORT_URL },
+    ],
+  },
+  {
+    id: 'startup-beta',
+    title: 'Startup installation beta',
+    uiLabels: ['Installation timing', 'Availability and deadline', 'Install after next restart · Beta', 'Startup install results', 'Beta', 'Check startup reports', 'Set up startup reporting', 'Edit shared policy', 'Apply to', 'Use shared policy', 'Save app exception', 'Not set up', 'Cancel before installation', 'Download cancellation script', 'Reports need review'],
+    flow: ['Choose a pilot app', 'Open Installation timing', 'Enable the startup Beta', 'Assign a Required pilot group', 'Deploy to Intune', 'Restart the pilot computer'],
+    points: [
+      "Beta: Windows pilot testing required. Local tests do not confirm real Intune delivery, Windows task permissions or restart installation. Validate staging, restart installation, reporting and cancellation on a pilot computer before wider rollout.",
+      "Install after next restart · Beta is opt-in for one app and off by default. Set it in the Installation timing section of the deploy panel or the Deployment list editor. Availability and deadline in Inventory only sets dates.",
+      "This sets the current deployment. Future automatic updates follow the shared policy or app exception in Installation timing on App updates.",
+      "For future automatic updates, open Edit shared policy in App updates. In Apply to choose the app, turn off Use shared policy, choose Install after next restart · Beta, then Save app exception. A tenant-wide restart rule is unavailable.",
+      "Choose a machine-wide MSI or EXE app and a Required assignment to a small pilot device group. Use a version not already detected as installed; an installed version is not installed again just to test the feature.",
+      "Store apps, per-user apps, ZIP/portable installers, custom detection, New computer setup and replacement of the previous version are excluded. The app explains restrictions before saving; the server also checks the saved and current deployment configuration.",
+      "The packaging workflow adds a startup coordinator to the selected package. GitHub Actions builds it and deploys it to Intune; installation runs on the computer. Deploy the matching workflow and its startup scripts before using the Beta.",
+      "Intune stages the package in a protected SYSTEM-owned folder. A SYSTEM startup task runs silently after a later Windows startup, delayed by a minute. It verifies staged files before running the original installer. Same-boot retries do not start installation.",
+      "The Beta does not force a restart, apply the three-day postponement rule, or block sign-in until installation finishes. Use Windows Restart for the pilot; sleep and resume do not count. A vendor installer may need network access or another restart.",
+      "The computer remembers deployment order. A delayed older package cannot replace a newer staged deployment for the same app. Delivery time and app version spelling do not decide which pending deployment runs.",
+      "Intune has no native waiting-for-startup installation state. Staging returns retry, never success just for staging. Intune may temporarily display retry or failure while waiting. Automatic detection checks the actual installed version.",
+      "Startup install results, the last row of the status strip in App updates, lists Beta packages when you press Open. It offers cancellation even when reporting isn't set up or can't be read.",
+      "Not set up means computers' startup results aren't collected yet; they show after the reporting script is assigned and connected. It does not mean installation succeeded.",
+      "Open Set up startup reporting for the detection-only Remediations script. Reporting requires eligible Microsoft Remediations licensing. Assign it to the pilot group, running as SYSTEM in 64-bit PowerShell hourly.",
+      "Grant the server app DeviceManagementScripts.Read.All with admin consent. Set HRM_STARTUP_REPORT_SCRIPT_ID on the server to the reporting script package ID. Install and enable the hourly startup-report collection timer on the server.",
+      "Check startup reports reads the latest Intune results; it does not force computers to check in. Counts cover received reports only. Computers without a report are never assumed installed. Last device activity and last collection time are shown separately. These reports are separate from Update history.",
+      "Reports distinguish Waiting for restart, Installing, Installed, Failed, Cancelled and Status out of date. Installed requires installer success and the version marker. It records an installation outcome, not live inventory. Short installations may finish between reporting runs.",
+      "Reports older than 6 hours, and Installing states older than 2 hours, need checking. Missing, malformed, omitted and unreadable results trigger Reports need review. If loading fails, the panel says Couldn't load startup reports and keeps the last successful snapshot. Press Check startup reports to try again.",
+      "The IT dashboard receives aggregate startup counts, including incomplete-report warnings. It never receives app, computer or staff names.",
+      "A slow installer keeps running while the coordinator waits. It is not stopped after an hour. Other startup installations wait up to 30 minutes, then retry at a later restart. Investigate an Installing state older than 2 hours.",
+      "Changing a policy, removing assignments or deleting the Intune app does not recall a staged package. An Uninstall assignment may not run before installation. Automatic old-copy cleanup leaves app families used with the Beta alone.",
+      "The local record is under ProgramData in IntuneGet-Startup-Beta. Successful installations remove their task and try to remove the staged payload, keeping status and deployment order. Returned installation failures can retry on later restarts up to 3 attempts.",
+      "A hung installer can keep other startup installations waiting until it exits or the computer restarts. An integrity error stops the task. Review installer logs before retrying a failure.",
+      "To retry a failed or cancelled package, deploy a new package; do not edit the protected local status.",
+      "To cancel, press Open on Startup install results in App updates, then Cancel before installation beside the package. Remove its Required assignment first. Press Download cancellation script and deploy it as a SYSTEM platform script to the pilot group.",
+      "The cancellation script blocks that exact pending package, including later delivery or retries, without uninstalling an installed app. If installation is running, wait for it to finish before retrying cancellation. Confirm Cancelled when reporting is connected.",
+    ],
+    links: [{ label: 'Open App updates', href: '/dashboard/updates#startup-installations' }, { label: 'Remediations licensing and setup', href: 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-management/tools/deploy-remediations' }],
+  },
+  {
+    id: 'automatic-installation-policy',
+    title: 'Installation timing for automatic updates',
+    uiLabels: ['App updates', 'Installation timing', 'Update schedule', 'Startup install results', 'Edit shared policy', 'Review app exceptions', 'Apply to', 'Use shared policy', 'When computers install', 'Let users install now or postpone', 'Days users can postpone', 'Install automatically', 'Use each app’s saved settings', 'Intune delivery', 'Open Inventory for install results', 'Save shared policy', 'Save app exception', 'Update history', 'Deployed to Intune', 'Inventory', 'Availability and deadline', 'Installation policy for this release', 'Install after next restart · Beta'],
+    flow: ['Open App updates', 'Edit shared policy', 'Choose when computers install', 'Choose days to postpone', 'Save shared policy'],
+    points: [
+      'The status strip at the top of App updates has 3 rows: Update schedule, Installation timing and Startup install results (see Startup installation beta).',
+      'Update schedule controls when new versions deploy to Intune. Installation timing controls prompts and postponement on computers for future automatic packages.',
+      'The Installation timing row shows the shared rule, such as Users can postpone · 3 days, and how many apps have exceptions. Open Review app exceptions to see or edit each one.',
+      'Press Edit shared policy to open the panel on the right. In Apply to, leave All apps with Automatic updates on selected to change the shared policy, or select one app to set an exception.',
+      'Under When computers install, choose Let users install now or postpone, then Days users can postpone. The form suggests 3 days. Save shared policy applies to future automatic update packages; Save app exception applies only to that app. Select Use shared policy to remove an exception.',
+      'Until a shared policy is saved, each app keeps its saved installation settings. Install automatically and Use each app’s saved settings are also available. Saving does not change queued or deployed packages, or computers that already have that version.',
+      'Postponement starts at each computer’s first install attempt, not when the server finds or deploys a version. Offline computers start later. After the period ends, the next Intune attempt installs the update; this is not an exact appointment.',
+      'Postponement uses the installer toolkit. It may install silently when nobody is signed in, during new-computer setup, or when the app does not need to close. A postponement is returned to Intune as retry; reminder times follow Intune checks, not a time chosen in this app.',
+      'If the app is in use after postponement expires, the package allows at least 5 minutes to close it. This is not a guarantee that the installer will preserve unsaved work or suppress a restart.',
+      'The Edit shared policy panel says which updates use the rule. Update, Update all and pilots keep their own saved settings. Packages already queued or deployed don’t change.',
+      'The panel also shows Intune delivery: computers install when Intune runs the assignment, with no fixed install time. Open Inventory for install results opens Inventory.',
+      'Update history records packaging and deploying. Deployed to Intune means the app reached Intune, not that computers installed it. Expand a deployed version for its install results and their last check time; Inventory also shows them.',
+      'Expanding a release also shows Installation policy for this release, the rule saved when packaging was queued. Postponement and deadlines on each computer are not reported in Update history.',
+      'Availability and deadline, in Inventory or on an App updates card, sets dates and restart grace on a copy already in Intune. Those dates belong to one assignment and do not carry over to future versions. Login-only installation is not offered.',
+      'The Installation timing section of the deploy panel sets the same package settings for one deployment, including Install after next restart · Beta for pilot testing. See Startup installation beta.',
+    ],
+    links: [{ label: 'Open App updates', href: '/dashboard/updates' }, { label: 'Open Inventory', href: '/dashboard/inventory' }],
+  },
+  {
+    id: 'update-schedule',
+    title: 'Update schedule and blackouts',
+    uiLabels: ["Update schedule", "Installation timing", "Auto-update", "Notify only", "Pause automatic updates", "Resume automatic updates", "Paused for all apps", "What does pausing change?", "Edit schedule", "Check for new versions", "Restrict deploying to selected days and hours", "Save schedule", "Waiting to deploy", "Add blackout", "Remove blackout", "Deploy to pilot group", "Approve wider rollout", "Update all", "Deploy new version"],
+    flow: ['Open App updates', 'Check Update schedule', 'Press Edit schedule', 'Set deployment hours', 'Save schedule'],
+    points: [
+      "Update schedule, the first row of the status strip in App updates, controls when new automatic update packages and pilots can deploy to Intune. It is shared across the tenant. Installation timing controls prompts and postponement on computers.",
+      "The row sums it up, for example Deploys to Intune Mon, Tue, Wed, Thu, Fri, 7 PM to 5 AM the next day, Maryland time. Its pill shows On, Waiting or Paused. Press Edit schedule for everything else.",
+      "The server checks for new versions at 3:30 AM and 3:30 PM, Maryland time, as Check for new versions in Edit schedule shows. It can deploy a new package for an app set to Auto-update only when automatic updates are allowed. These are not computer installation times.",
+      "Choose Auto-update on an app to deploy its new versions automatically. Choose Notify only to review that app manually. Changing one app’s rule does not pause other apps.",
+      "Pause automatic updates, at the top of Edit schedule, holds new automatic updates, pilot starts and wider rollouts across the tenant. Paused for all apps confirms it. Open What does pausing change? for scope: checks and queued work can continue.",
+      "While paused, the row shows Paused and its own Resume automatic updates button. Resume automatic updates removes the shared pause. Deployment hours, blackouts and per-app update rules still apply. Resuming does not override those restrictions or change Installation timing.",
+      "To set hours, press Edit schedule. Select Restrict deploying to selected days and hours, choose days and hours in Maryland time, then press Save schedule.",
+      "Leaving Restrict deploying to selected days and hours unchecked means no time restriction. It does not turn Auto-update off. Use Pause automatic updates to hold all new automatic updates, or Notify only for one app.",
+      "An end hour before the start hour runs past midnight, for example 8 PM to 6 AM.",
+      "Updates found outside the allowed hours wait. The row shows Waiting with the reason, and Edit schedule says Waiting to deploy. Within an hour of the schedule opening, the server can start held updates if nothing is paused and no blackout applies.",
+      "A blackout holds automatic updates and pilots on whole dates, whatever the hours say. In Edit schedule, enter a short name, the first day and the last day, then press Add blackout. Use Remove blackout to end one early.",
+      "Name blackouts after the work, such as Month-end close. Never put client or staff details in the name. A blackout ends at midnight Maryland time after its last day. Past blackouts drop off the list. The row says how many are current or upcoming.",
+      "Without restricted hours, updates held by a blackout can start at the first 3:30 AM or 3:30 PM check after it ends, provided automatic updates have not also been paused.",
+      "Update, Update all and Deploy new version on App updates, and deploying from the App catalog, still work at any time as manual overrides. Pausing does not cancel packages already queued or installations already delivered to computers.",
+      "While automatic updates are paused, outside allowed hours or during a blackout, Deploy to pilot group and Approve wider rollout cannot proceed. A pilot already testing keeps its pilot group. Check the displayed reason before trying again.",
+    ],
+    links: [{ label: 'Open App updates', href: '/dashboard/updates' }],
+  },
+  {
+    id: 'rollback',
+    title: 'Going back to a previous version',
+    uiLabels: ['Inventory', 'Actions', 'Previous version', 'Go back to previous version', 'I checked the versions and groups', 'Allow again', 'Check installer now', 'Needs attention', 'Deployments'],
+    flow: ['Open the app in Inventory', 'Press Go back to previous version', 'Check versions and groups', 'Press Go back'],
+    points: [
+      'If a new version causes problems, open the app in Inventory. Under Actions, find Previous version and press Go back to previous version.',
+      'It appears on the newest copy IntuneGet deployed, when IntuneGet also deployed an older version of the app. Custom apps can go back too. Self-updating apps can\'t.',
+      'Under Previous version, a line shows whether the earlier installer still downloads and when it was checked. IntuneGet checks the saved link at the 3:30 AM and 3:30 PM update checks, Maryland time, without downloading the file.',
+      'Each of these checks stops after 1 minute and starts with the apps checked longest ago. With many apps, some are checked at the next update check instead.',
+      'Press Check installer now to check the link, then download the earlier installer once and compare it with the checksum IntuneGet saved. The file is not kept. The download starts only if the link answers.',
+      'Check installer now doesn\'t show when IntuneGet saved no checksum for the earlier installer.',
+      'A large installer can take several minutes. The line updates when the check finishes, and Go back to previous version is hidden until then. The server runs at most 2 of these checks at a time.',
+      'If the earlier installer no longer downloads, has changed, or its saved link can\'t be used, Go back to previous version is hidden and Home lists the app under Needs attention. Fix a bad update with a newer version instead.',
+      'IntuneGet only downloads https links on public internet hosts. If the saved link is anything else, going back to that version from IntuneGet isn\'t possible.',
+      'A changed installer stays blocked until Check installer now finds a match. A missing one clears when a later check finds the file again.',
+      'If the installer couldn\'t be reached, for example because the download site didn\'t answer or its name couldn\'t be found, you can still open the review. It checks the link again and stops if it still can\'t reach it.',
+      'The review checks the installer link again and reads the app and its groups from Intune. Check both versions and the groups, select I checked the versions and groups, then press Go back to v[version].',
+      'IntuneGet deploys the earlier version again from the installer it used before, as a new copy. It replaces the current copy, so Intune uninstalls the current version and then installs the earlier one.',
+      'The groups move from the current copy to the new one, as with an update. Nothing changes in Intune until packaging finishes, which takes a few minutes. Follow it in Deployments.',
+      'Before going back, check that the earlier version is safe to reinstall. A matching checksum only shows the file is unchanged. Some apps keep settings or data that an older version can\'t read.',
+      'Automatic updates then skip the version you went back from. A newer version still updates automatically. Update or Update all on App updates can still deploy it on purpose.',
+      'The version stays skipped even if going back fails.',
+      'To let automatic updates deploy a skipped version again, press Allow again next to it under Previous version.',
+      'Going back is refused while a pilot or another deployment for the app is running, or while the app is being retired.',
+      'Going back doesn\'t need the old copy in Intune, so it still works after IntuneGet removes old copies (see How old copies are removed).',
+    ],
+    links: [{ label: 'Open Inventory', href: '/dashboard/inventory' }, { label: 'Open Deployments', href: '/dashboard/uploads' }],
+  },
+  {
+    id: 'retire',
+    title: 'Retiring an app',
+    uiLabels: ['Inventory', 'Actions', 'Retire app', 'I understand this uninstalls the app from these groups', 'Uninstall from these groups', 'Start retiring', 'Retiring this app', 'Removing this app', 'Discovered apps', 'Remove from computers', 'Delete from Intune', 'Keep it for now', 'Delete even though computers still report it', 'Cancel retiring', 'Ignore', 'Ignored apps'],
+    flow: ['Open the app in Inventory', 'Press Retire app', 'Check the groups', 'Uninstall from these groups', 'Wait for computers', 'Delete from Intune'],
